## Claim

$$\text{Hom}_{\text{Grp}}(\mathbb{Q},\,\mathbb{Z}) \;=\; \{0\},$$

i.e. the only group homomorphism $(\mathbb{Q},+)\to(\mathbb{Z},+)$ is the zero homomorphism.

## Proof

Let $f:\mathbb{Q}\to\mathbb{Z}$ be a homomorphism of additive groups. We show $f=0$.

Fix an arbitrary $x\in\mathbb{Q}$. For every positive integer $n\ge 1$, the element $x/n$ is well-defined in $\mathbb{Q}$ and satisfies

$$x \;=\; n\cdot\frac{x}{n}.$$

Applying $f$ and using that $f$ is additive (hence $\mathbb{Z}$-linear on the additive group, i.e. $f(n\cdot y)=n\cdot f(y)$ for $y\in\mathbb{Q}$, $n\in\mathbb{Z}$):

$$f(x) \;=\; f\!\left(n\cdot\frac{x}{n}\right) \;=\; n\cdot f\!\left(\frac{x}{n}\right).$$

Since $f(x/n)\in\mathbb{Z}$, this shows

$$f(x)\in n\,\mathbb{Z}\qquad\text{for every }n\ge 1.$$

In particular $f(x)$ is divisible by **every** positive integer $n$. The only integer with this property is $0$: if $m\in\mathbb{Z}$ and $m\neq 0$, then $|m|\ge 1$, and taking $n=|m|+1$ gives $m\notin n\mathbb{Z}$ (since $0<|m|<n$). Hence $f(x)=0$.

Since $x\in\mathbb{Q}$ was arbitrary, $f\equiv 0$.

Conversely, the zero map is indeed a homomorphism. Therefore the only element of $\text{Hom}_{\text{Grp}}(\mathbb{Q},\mathbb{Z})$ is the zero homomorphism, and

$$\boxed{\;\text{Hom}_{\text{Grp}}(\mathbb{Q},\,\mathbb{Z})\;\cong\;0\;}$$

as an abelian group (under pointwise addition of homomorphisms).

